      "text": "Roman rhetoric is Greek rhetoric institutionalized, expanded, and systematized for a society in which public speaking was the path to political power. Cicero and Quintilian are not just important figures in rhetorical history — they are the defining voices of Western humanist education for 1,500 years."
    },
    {
      "id": "02-ROMAN-RHETORIC.md#003",
      "path": [
        "02-ROMAN-RHETORIC.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:rhetoric:02-roman-rhetoric, git-history:rhetoric:02-roman-rhetoric]",
        "canonical_path": "rhetoric/02-ROMAN-RHETORIC.md",
        "concepts": "[roman, rhetoric]",
        "current_path": "rhetoric/02-ROMAN-RHETORIC.md",
        "id": "maxim:rhetoric:roman-rhetoric",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "rhetoric",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[roman, rhetoric]",
        "section": "rhetoric",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Roman Rhetoric",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                 ROMAN RHETORIC — LANDSCAPE                       |\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                                                                  |\n|  CICERO (106-43 BCE)                                             |\n|  De Inventione (early): theory of argument discovery            |\n|  De Oratore (55 BCE): the mature synthesis; the ideal orator   |\n|  Orator (46 BCE): the orator's style across three levels        |\n|  Brutus (46 BCE): history of Roman oratory                      |\n|  The Philippics: his political speeches (killed him)            |\n|                                                                  |\n|  QUINTILIAN (c. 35-100 CE)                                      |\n|  Institutio Oratoria: the complete education of an orator       |\n|  12 books; comprehensive; the ideal orator is also a good man  |\n|                                                                  |\n|  ROMAN FORENSIC PRACTICE                                         |\n|  The Roman courts: advocacy as political career                  |\n|  The stasis theory: finding the point at issue                  |\n|  Actio (delivery) as core political performance                  |\n|                                                                  |\n|  THE LEGACY                                                      |\n|  Medieval ars praedicandi (preaching manuals)                   |\n|  Renaissance humanism (studia humanitatis)                      |\n|  Jesuit education (rhetorical training)                         |\n|  Modern business communication (trace the lineage)             |\n+------------------------------------------------------------------+\n```"
    },
