        "root_concepts": "[roman, rhetoric]",
        "section": "rhetoric",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Roman Rhetoric",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nDE ORATORE (On the Orator, 55 BCE)\n------------------------------------\nA dialogue set in 91 BCE at Crassus's villa.\nParticipants: Crassus (Cicero's model), Antonius, others.\nFormat: the dialogue form allows Cicero to present\n  multiple positions without committing to one — very\n  Platonic in structure; un-Platonic in content."
    },
    {
      "id": "02-ROMAN-RHETORIC.md#009",
      "path": [
        "02-ROMAN-RHETORIC.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:rhetoric:02-roman-rhetoric, git-history:rhetoric:02-roman-rhetoric]",
        "canonical_path": "rhetoric/02-ROMAN-RHETORIC.md",
        "concepts": "[roman, rhetoric]",
        "current_path": "rhetoric/02-ROMAN-RHETORIC.md",
        "id": "maxim:rhetoric:roman-rhetoric",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "rhetoric",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[roman, rhetoric]",
        "section": "rhetoric",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Roman Rhetoric",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "THE CENTRAL QUESTION:\n  What is the ideal orator?\n  The dialogue is a negotiation between:\n    Crassus: the ideal orator must have BROAD PHILOSOPHICAL KNOWLEDGE.\n    Antonius: practical skill and experience are sufficient."
    },
    {
      "id": "02-ROMAN-RHETORIC.md#010",
      "path": [
        "02-ROMAN-RHETORIC.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:rhetoric:02-roman-rhetoric, git-history:rhetoric:02-roman-rhetoric]",
        "canonical_path": "rhetoric/02-ROMAN-RHETORIC.md",
        "concepts": "[roman, rhetoric]",
        "current_path": "rhetoric/02-ROMAN-RHETORIC.md",
        "id": "maxim:rhetoric:roman-rhetoric",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "rhetoric",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[roman, rhetoric]",
        "section": "rhetoric",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Roman Rhetoric",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Cicero (via Crassus) wins: the ideal orator combines\n  philosophical wisdom (to know the truth) with rhetorical\n  skill (to present it effectively).\n  Neither the philosopher who cannot speak persuasively\n  nor the clever debater without genuine knowledge is ideal."
    },
    {
      "id": "02-ROMAN-RHETORIC.md#011",
      "path": [
        "02-ROMAN-RHETORIC.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:rhetoric:02-roman-rhetoric, git-history:rhetoric:02-roman-rhetoric]",
        "canonical_path": "rhetoric/02-ROMAN-RHETORIC.md",
        "concepts": "[roman, rhetoric]",
        "current_path": "rhetoric/02-ROMAN-RHETORIC.md",
        "id": "maxim:rhetoric:roman-rhetoric",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "rhetoric",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[roman, rhetoric]",
        "section": "rhetoric",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Roman Rhetoric",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "THE IDEAL ORATOR'S QUALIFICATIONS:\n  1. Knowledge of the subject (you cannot speak well about\n     what you don't understand)\n  2. Philosophical training (ethics, politics, psychology)\n  3. Command of all three styles (plain/middle/grand)\n  4. Mastery of all five canons\n  5. Natural talent + training + practice"
    },
    {
      "id": "02-ROMAN-RHETORIC.md#012",
      "path": [
        "02-ROMAN-RHETORIC.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[proof-backfill:rhetoric:02-roman-rhetoric, git-history:rhetoric:02-roman-rhetoric]",
        "canonical_path": "rhetoric/02-ROMAN-RHETORIC.md",
        "concepts": "[roman, rhetoric]",
        "current_path": "rhetoric/02-ROMAN-RHETORIC.md",
        "id": "maxim:rhetoric:roman-rhetoric",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "rhetoric",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[roman, rhetoric]",
        "section": "rhetoric",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Roman Rhetoric",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "CICERO ON ELOQUENCE:\n  \"Sed quid ego de utilitate loquor? Etiam si summo studio\n  colitur [eloquentia], tamen...pauci hunc cursum conficere\n  potuerunt.\"\n  \"Eloquence is the most powerful force in human affairs.\n  Its mastery is rare; it changes nations.\"\n  This is not boasting — it is what Cicero observed in\n  the Roman Republic: the best speeches changed votes,\n  freed innocent men, condemned the guilty, and moved\n  the Senate.\n```"
    },
    {
